t raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3:$\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{3}=\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{7}^{3}}\n$$\n\nNow, using ${3}^{3}=27$ and ${7}^{3}=343,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{3}^{3}}{{7}^{3}}=\\frac{27}{343}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d87a38cc-5ca0-534e-8fa1-0a6e68d65706" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Powers of Fractions Using Power of Quotient Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3a6092-0ccc-5ace-b0a2-bbc9a1d476ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544113} {:answer/id #uuid "131b820a-255c-5c25-b591-253c1c2de483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544129} {:answer/id #uuid "24a253e9-25f8-58a3-a4ca-11ccea22f01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544167} {:answer/id #uuid "2fec3986-8b5e-5f78-b80f-5ca09b68480c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544192} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebc8797-8c31-5b8e-8e34-21f6a5e5a7b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ebc8797-8c31-5b8e-8e34-21f6a5e5a7b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544331} :answer-field/id #uuid "4da087de-bf0c-5ba2-9cf1-aeb4a1fe7f57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1fb4db83-3d4f-5c8b-938d-159db1a747a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-68721" :question/problem "$10{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3.$ So we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}=\\frac{8}{125}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n10{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=10\\cdot \\frac{8}{125}=\\frac{80}{125}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{80}{125}=\\frac{16\\cdot 5}{25\\cdot 5}=\\frac{16}{25}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105744 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46cd136a-cc20-51e1-ac90-f8d9b787c5b3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Powers of Negative Fractions Using the Power of Quotient Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283aec17-150c-5d77-97de-8e2a579c1b9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544179} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6c5baf-6923-5afe-bd84-66fa2b5ce9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544188} {:answer/id #uuid "98102fa6-60fa-5c9e-a3d6-b1c5223c3f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544417} {:answer/id #uuid "be6a372e-767d-5043-93bc-06652273b513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544492} {:answer/id #uuid "fae39428-4f93-5219-a733-bb8b7cbb74a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98102fa6-60fa-5c9e-a3d6-b1c5223c3f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544417} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c6a5509-5ce2-50bc-ada9-2d5121a407c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a48af0b4-b2cb-5194-ba80-dd04350d6f6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-68731" :question/problem "${(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $-\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(-2)}{3}.$\n\nTo raise the fraction to the power of $4$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $4:$\n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}={(\\frac{(-2)}{3})}^{4}=\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{{3}^{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, using $(-2)^{4}=16$ and ${3}^{4}=81,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{{3}^{4}}=\\frac{16}{81}.\n$$"}]}
